==================================================
7. QUANTITIES AND MEASUREMENTS
==================================================

Support three tracking modes:
1. Exact.
2. Approximate.
3. Presence only.

Examples:
- Exactly 24 buttons.
- Approximately 20 beads.
- About half a bottle.
- A small bag of scraps.
- I own this, quantity not tracked.

Unknown is not zero.
Do not invent numeric quantities from descriptive amounts.

Store measurement value, unit, and meaning separately.
Avoid ambiguous “Size: 5” fields.

Support suitable units for:
- Count.
- Length.
- Mass.
- Volume.
- Width/height/thickness/diameter.

Use safe decimal representations and defined conversion rules.
Convert only within compatible dimensions.
Do not convert mass to length or volume without explicit, valid item-specific information.

Support metric and imperial display preferences.
Do not alter the meaning of stored measurements when preferences change.

Do not automatically combine:
- Different dye lots.
- Different storage locations.
- Incompatible units.
- Distinct fabric pieces.
- Opened and unopened containers when the user tracks them separately.

Fabric example:
One supply may have:
- Piece A: 24 × 36 inches.
- Piece B: 8 × 10 inches.

Do not claim these are equivalent to a single continuous piece with the same total area.

