==================================================
11. PROJECT REQUIREMENTS, MATCHING, AND ALLOCATION
==================================================

A project requirement is not the same object as a stash item.

Example:
Requirement:
“200 g of worsted-weight cotton yarn”

Selected stash allocation:
“Blue cotton yarn, batch A”

Match status:
“User-confirmed substitute”

Requirement fields may include:
- Description.
- Category.
- Material.
- Color preference.
- Required quantity/unit.
- Required dimensions.
- Required attributes.
- Whether a single continuous piece is required.
- Whether multiple batches may be combined.
- Whether mixing dye lots is acceptable.
- Consumable versus reusable tool.
- Optional/required.

Users must be able to approve, reject, replace, or manually link a possible match.

Material states:
- Have enough.
- Have some.
- Missing.
- Quantity unknown.
- Possible substitute; review needed.

Do not label a project “Ready to make” unless all required structured requirements have confirmed coverage.
For incomplete material lists, show:
“Coverage applies to the requirements you entered.”

Never infer that matching category names alone proves suitability.

==================================================
12. RESERVATIONS AND CONSUMPTION
==================================================

Distinguish:
- On hand.
- Reserved.
- Available.

Calculate numeric availability only when compatible, sufficiently precise quantities exist.
For approximate or unknown quantities, use honest labels instead of false precision.

Reservations:
- Are optional.
- Do not physically consume inventory.
- Warn when the same quantity is overcommitted.
- Do not silently create negative stock.
- Show which projects reserve a batch.

Reusable tools:
- Can be linked to multiple projects.
- Are not consumed.
- May show a nonblocking “also used by another project” note if useful.

Completing a project:
- Ask whether to record actual usage.
- Let users change the amount used.
- Preserve leftovers.
- Allow skipping consumption updates.
- Do not automatically consume planned amounts.

Use atomic database transactions for allocation and usage updates.
Keep a minimal usage history so mistakes can be reversed reliably.
Undo must restore related quantities consistently.

Archiving or deleting a project must explicitly handle its reservations without deleting the physical supplies.

==================================================
13. IDEAS: SAVED-PROJECT MATCHING FIRST
==================================================

The Ideas screen must prioritize the user’s own projects.

Entry points:
- Open Ideas from navigation.
- Select “Get ideas” on one supply.
- Select several supplies and choose “Get ideas.”

Allow:
- Use any selected supply.
- Try to use all selected supplies.

Do not confuse these modes.

Primary sections:
- Saved projects using these supplies.
- Projects with confirmed material coverage.
- Projects with only one or a few known missing requirements.
- Possible substitutions to review.
- Projects with uncertain quantities.

Prefer active idea/planned/paused projects.
Let users include completed or archived projects explicitly.

Matching must run locally and deterministically.
No LLM required.

Rank using transparent criteria such as:
- Confirmed links to selected supplies.
- Satisfaction of entered requirements.
- Number of known missing requirements.
- Unknown requirements/quantities.
- User-confirmed substitutions.

Do not present arbitrary confidence percentages.
Explain why a project appears:
- “Uses your cotton yarn.”
- “3 of 4 entered materials confirmed.”
- “Button quantity not tracked.”
- “Possible fabric match; dimensions need review.”

A single batch must not be counted twice toward separate requirements unless sufficient available quantity exists.
Account for reservations and compatibility rules.

Free-text-only requirements should not produce unjustified exact matches.
Allow “possible match” suggestions and user confirmation.

If there are no saved-project matches, say so clearly and offer inspiration alternatives.
Do not manufacture saved projects.

==================================================
14. IDEAS: LOCAL INSPIRATION AND WEB SEARCHES
==================================================

Secondary inspiration functionality must work without generative AI.

Use a small, editable, locally bundled set of rules connecting supply attributes to broad project categories.

Examples:
- Fabric scraps → patchwork, appliqué, small pouches.
- Ribbon remnants → bookmarks, decorative tassels, gift embellishments.
- Beads and suitable findings → charms, stitch markers, jewelry.
- Yarn remnants → small crochet/knit accessories, colorwork, decorative projects.

These are idea starters, not validated patterns.
Do not claim selected quantities are sufficient.

Avoid safety-sensitive project recommendations such as:
- Food-contact items from unspecified materials.
- Heat-resistant items from unverified fibers/materials.
- Baby or pet products involving unassessed choking, entanglement, or toxicity risks.

Offer filters/constraints:
- Craft type.
- Beginner-friendly.
- Small project.
- Scrap/remnant friendly.
- No sewing machine.
- Approximate time preference.
- Intended use: decorative, gift, accessory, home organization.
- Prefer using selected supplies.

If a constraint cannot be established from local information, label it as a search preference rather than a verified property.

Generate editable search phrases locally.
Examples:
- “small crochet projects cotton yarn leftovers”
- “beginner hand sewing projects fabric scraps”
- “ribbon remnant bookmark ideas”
- “beaded stitch marker tutorial”
- “cross stitch small motifs leftover floss”

Do not add exact quantities or characteristics unless the selected records support them.

Show the full query before opening an external destination.
Allow editing and copying.
Do not silently include private notes, locations, or the entire inventory.

